    "Keep span topology, table transfer and width policy with the feature owner while mounted host lifetime follows the exact view.",
    "A public API or lower primitive must remove proven caller work or own a neutral substrate law; a hypothetical consumer does not earn public surface.",
    "Runtime replacement must preserve same-key host rebinding, cleanup, independent views and zero cell-render fan-out under direct scale comparison.",
    "Review and decision artifacts only; no product implementation, implementation plan, publication or release."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "The latest record and all table source fingerprints were matching. Re-read current Plate table public types, headless selection helpers, React interaction, copied UI, Plite NodeSelection mapping and mounted node binding. Reused the pinned seven-repository research observations, actual-hook replacement probe and rejected rendered-attribute benchmark because their questions and hashes still match. Four independent skeptical source reviews challenged selection promotion, resize consolidation, paint ownership and the whole direction. No external discovery or product test was repeated.",
  "summary": "Pursue one substrate correction and narrow the table redesign. Plite React should project its built-in NodeSelection onto exact mounted hosts with neutral selected/anchor attributes, then Plate should delete useTableSelectionDOM. Stop the proposed public table.update.select API: current behavior has one integration and no headless column-selection consumer. Keep the existing resize math, transaction, pointer-session and preview owners; defer replacing per-cell hit targets until a matched browser benchmark wins. No table selection kind, grid, controller or resize API moves into Plite.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-17-table-substrate-ownership",
  "relation": "supersedes",
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep the table painter: rejected as the durable target because it duplicates NodeSelection-to-host binding and demonstrably misses same-key host replacement.",
    "Repair the table hook locally: viable fallback, but rejected as the first target because Plite already owns NodeSelection and every mounted node binding. Use it only if the neutral Plite candidate fails its behavior or scale gate.",
    "Promote semantic table selection writes: stop. React already reuses private headless topology helpers; copied UI uses ordinary ranges; no current column-selection consumer justifies public API debt. Reassess explicit selectCells/selectRow/selectColumn commands only with a real second caller.",
    "Add a Plite NodeSelection DOM projection: pursue as an internal mounted-view law with literal neutral selected and anchor attributes. Add no public host registry or generic attribute API.",
    "Reuse Plate rendered attributes: reject the measured implementation because it caused 15,045/60,045 cell renders and material p95 regressions. Do not reintroduce that fan-out.",
    "Replace per-cell resize targets: defer. Existing lifecycle ownership is already consolidated; source element counts do not prove cost, and upstream architectures disagree. A disposable table-scoped candidate must win a matched browser comparison.",
    "Move table semantics or resizing into Plite: reject. Keep span geometry, transfer, width redistribution and resize calculation in Plate table; keep the private pointer helper in Plate React.",
    "Add a custom table selection kind, public controller/grid package or spreadsheet model: reject because exact NodeSelection membership and the existing rich-cell document model satisfy current jobs."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Source and immutable evidence review only. The matching Happy DOM probe still passes 11 controls then fails selected/anchor paint after same-key td-to-th replacement. Existing stable-host proof remains valid. The Plite host-projection candidate is unbuilt and unmeasured; no exact-browser paint, multi-view cleanup run, candidate scale benchmark, physical device, upstream test execution or product change occurred. Resize wrapper/handle counts and the merged-left-handle defect are source-derived; no resize-control performance claim is made. Prior adopted canonical table behavior remains adopted while proof stays partial for the reproduced host-replacement gap.",
